{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"S2 (e042) ReconcilerSettings captures `comment_max_chars` and `jira_cli_timeout` at compose, but neither is threaded into the built backend: the DC sanitizer's `comment_max_chars()` still calls `resolve_comment_max_chars()` ambiently on first use, and EVERY Cloud ACLI subprocess call still resolves `reconciler.jira_cli_timeout` ambiently inside `_run_acli` (`_acli_call_timeout()`). Complete the operation-scoped capture for these two fields. Advisory finding from Gerrit 1843 PS1 LLM-Review.\n\n## Approach\n\n**DC `comment_max_chars`:**\n- `JiraDataCenterBackend.__init__` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py): when a captured `scope` is present, construct `self.sanitizer = _DCSanitizer(comment_max_chars=scope.comment_max_chars)` via the existing injection seam; `scope is None` keeps the legacy lazy ambient resolve. `<= 0` already means UNLIMITED in `_truncate_dc_comment_body`, matching `resolve_dc_comment_max_chars`'s 0 convention, so the captured value threads verbatim.\n\n**Cloud `jira_cli_timeout` — thread through EVERY ACLI subprocess chokepoint, not just `AcliClient._run`.** The enumerated `_run_acli` call sites that bypass `_run` (each already threads `acli_cmd` the same way; `call_timeout` rides alongside it):\n- `acli_subprocess._run_acli` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_subprocess.py) gains `call_timeout: int | None = None`; a positive value bounds `communicate(timeout=...)`, non-positive/`None` falls back to the existing ambient `_acli_call_timeout()` via a tiny `_effective_call_timeout` helper (keeps `_run_acli`'s McCabe score flat), so direct/legacy constructions stay byte-identical.\n- `acli_cli_ops` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py): `call_timeout: int | None = None` added and forwarded through the whole create/read/comment call chain that spawns ACLI — `_create_issue_no_json` (direct `_run_acli`), `_create_from_json_payload` (direct), `_create_issue_from_json`, `create_issue`, `_verify_created_issue`, `get_issue` (direct), `add_comment` (direct), `get_comments` (direct).\n- `acli.py` module-level `update_issue` gains `call_timeout: int | None = None` forwarded to its direct `_run_acli` WRITE.\n- `AcliClient.__init__` gains keyword `call_timeout: int = 0`, stored as `self._call_timeout`; `_run` forwards it to `_run_acli`, and every delegation that bypasses `_run` passes it explicitly: `create_issue` -> `acli_cli_ops.create_issue`, `update_issue` -> module `update_issue`, `get_issue` -> `acli_cli_ops.get_issue`, `add_comment` -> `acli_cli_ops.add_comment`, and `delete_issue`'s direct `_run_acli`. (`AcliClient.get_comments` and the graph mixin already route through `self._run`.)\n- `ReconcilerRuntime._build_cloud` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/runtime.py): pass `call_timeout=s.jira_cli_timeout` when building the real `AcliClient`.\n\n**Explicit scope-outs (stated, intentional):** `update_priority`, `transition_issue`, `unassign_issue` and the `_rest_urlopen_with_retry`/direct-REST paths spawn no ACLI subprocess — their fixed urlopen timeouts are not `jira_cli_timeout` and are unchanged. Legacy module-level entry points invoked without a `call_timeout` (no composed runtime) keep the ambient `_acli_call_timeout()` resolve — that is the compatibility floor, not a gap in the operation-scoped capture.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A DC backend built with a captured scope truncates comments at the scope's `comment_max_chars` without consulting ambient config — RED-first test monkeypatches `resolve_comment_max_chars` to return a different value and asserts the scope value wins; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py -q`.\n- [ ] A DC backend built WITHOUT a scope keeps the legacy lazy ambient resolve — existing bug-049e coverage stays green; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler -k \"dc or datacenter\" -q`.\n- [ ] `_run_acli(call_timeout=N)` bounds the subprocess wait at N seconds while unset/0/None falls back to `_acli_call_timeout()` — pinned by unit tests; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py -q`.\n- [ ] Every enumerated bypass site forwards the captured timeout: an `AcliClient(call_timeout=N)` create/update/get/comment/delete call reaches `_run_acli` with `call_timeout=N` (no ambient resolve) — asserted by a RED-first test monkeypatching `acli_subprocess._run_acli` and recording the kwarg across all five client methods; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py -q`.\n- [ ] `ReconcilerRuntime._build_cloud` threads the captured `jira_cli_timeout` into the constructed `AcliClient` — asserted in the runtime bindings test; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py -q`.\n- [ ] Gates pass: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` (new=0 increased=0) all exit 0.\n\n## Testing\n\nRED-first: add failing tests in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py` (scope-threaded DC sanitizer ceiling; `AcliClient` `call_timeout` forwarding across `create_issue`/`update_issue`/`get_issue`/`add_comment`/`delete_issue` via a recording `_run_acli` monkeypatch; `_build_cloud` wiring), confirm they fail, then implement until green. `_run_acli`'s own bound/fallback behaviour is tested where its timeout tests live (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py`). Regression: `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler -q`, plus `make lint` and `make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787440101312224001,"uuid":"4cb9ee36-1cce-41f2-978c-193231a03d06"}